{
  "_comment": "The cross-language node-registry parity fixture (TASK-734 Task 3, extended by TASK-720 Task 5 with the five summarization-palette node types, by TASK-724 Task 3 with the eight STT-palette node types — stt.phiHop is implemented:false, a documented placeholder pending TASK-710 — and by TASK-731 Task 9 with the consultation palette. The consultation palette now carries ALL THIRTEEN node types of docs/implementation/TASK-731-Palette-Consultation/contracts/node-types.md's node table: TASK-731 shipped three (consentGate, phiHop, hitlGate) and left ten specified-but-unwired, which made the palette unbuildable because DRAFT_CONSULTATION_RULE_SET names nine node types by key; the remaining ten interpreter wrappers were added later over the compile targets palette-contract.md §1 had already verified. consultation.hitlGate remains implemented:false, a documented placeholder pending the interpreter's own durable-wait extension (Phase B), so compile() still refuses any graph containing it. Asserted from BOTH packages/workflow-contract/src/__tests__/node-registry-parity.test.ts (TS) and apps/harness/src/harness/tests/unit/temporal/interpreter/test_node_registry_parity.py (Python) — see packages/workflow-contract/src/node-registry.ts's module docstring for why the two sides check against this file instead of against each other. Also carries the two palette-agnostic GRAPH BOUNDARY MARKERS `core.start`/`core.end` (TS class `boundary`): the palette-independent structural rules WF-S-002/003/004/007 are written against these literal node types, so until they were registered no graph in any palette could validate — the platform's own seeded summarization graph scored 17 errors against its own validator. They execute nothing but are `implemented: true` because compile() refuses any graph containing an unimplemented type. `entries` sorted by `key`. Only fields BOTH registries carry today are listed here; `classes`/`paletteKey` (TS-only) and `kind` (Python-only) are intentionally excluded.",
